 {"startPos": 1051,
  "moduleName": "Mathlib.Init.Set",
  "kind": "definition",
  "fullName": "Set",
  "endPos": 1054,
  "detail":
  "```lean\nSet.{u} (α : Type u) : Type u\n```\n***\nA set is a collection of elements of some type `α`.\n\nAlthough `Set` is defined as `α → Prop`, this is an implementation detail which should not be\nrelied on. Instead, `setOf` and membership of a set (`∈`) should be used to convert between sets\nand predicates.\n\n***\n*import Mathlib.Init.Set*",
  "definitionStartPos": {"line": 58, "column": 4},
  "definitionPath":
  "/Users/hattoriseiji/Autoinformalization/ExportInfos/./.lake/packages/mathlib//Mathlib/Init/Set.lean",
  "definitionEndPos": {"line": 58, "column": 7}},
 {"startPos": 1040,
  "moduleName": "Mathlib.Init.Set",
  "kind": "definition",
  "fullName": "Set.univ",
  "endPos": 1048,
  "detail":
  "```lean\nSet.univ.{u} {α : Type u} : Set α\n```\n***\nThe universal set on a type `α` is the set containing all elements of `α`.\n\nThis is conceptually the \"same as\" `α` (in set theory, it is actually the same), but type theory\nmakes the distinction that `α` is a type while `Set.univ` is a term of type `Set α`. `Set.univ` can\nitself be coerced to a type `↥Set.univ` which is in bijection with (but distinct from) `α`. \n***\n*import Mathlib.Init.Set*",
  "definitionStartPos": {"line": 164, "column": 4},
  "definitionPath":
  "/Users/hattoriseiji/Autoinformalization/ExportInfos/./.lake/packages/mathlib//Mathlib/Init/Set.lean",
  "definitionEndPos": {"line": 164, "column": 8}},
